Article 13 Financial institutions and non-bank payment institutions shall perform the following obligations for preventing illegal fundraising:
(1) Establish and improve internal management systems, prohibit branches and employees from participating in illegal fundraising, and prevent others from using their business premises or sales channels to engage in illegal fundraising;
(2) Strengthen public education on preventing illegal fundraising, and set up warning signs in prominent locations in their business premises;
(3) Strictly implement the reporting system for large-value transactions and suspicious transactions according to law, analyze and identify accounts related to abnormal capital flows suspected of illegal fundraising, and promptly report the relevant information to the branches and dispatched offices of the financial regulatory departments of the State Council in the locality and the leading department for handling illegal fundraising.

Article 19 With respect to the following acts within its administrative region that are suspected of illegal fundraising, the leading department for handling illegal fundraising shall promptly organize the relevant industry competent authorities, regulatory authorities, and branches and dispatched offices of the financial regulatory departments of the State Council to conduct investigation and determination:
(1) Establishing internet enterprises, investment and investment consulting enterprises, various trading venues or platforms, farmers' professional cooperatives, mutual fund organizations, or other organizations to absorb funds;
(2) Absorbing funds under the guise of issuing or transferring equity or debt, raising funds, selling insurance products, or engaging in various types of asset management, virtual currency, financial leasing, or other business;
(3) Absorbing funds in commercial activities such as selling goods, providing services, or investing in projects by promising to give returns in the form of currency, equity, or physical objects;
(4) Disseminating information about absorbing funds publicly through mass media, instant messaging tools, or other means in violation of laws, administrative regulations, or relevant state provisions;
(5) Other acts suspected of illegal fundraising.

Article 21 When organizing the investigation of suspected illegal fundraising activities, the leading department for handling illegal fundraising may take the following measures:
(1) Enter the premises suspected of being used for illegal fundraising to conduct investigation and evidence collection;
(2) Question the entities and individuals related to the investigated incident and require them to explain relevant matters;
(3) Review and copy documents, materials, electronic data, etc., related to the investigated incident, and seal up documents, materials, electronic devices, etc., that may be transferred, concealed, or destroyed;
(4) With the approval of the principal head of the leading department for handling illegal fundraising, inquire about relevant accounts suspected of being involved in illegal fundraising according to law.
There shall be at least two investigators, and they shall present their law enforcement credentials.
The entities and individuals related to the investigated incident shall cooperate with the investigation and shall not refuse or obstruct it.

Article 24 As required by the handling of illegal fundraising, the leading department for handling illegal fundraising may take the following measures:
(1) Seal up the relevant business premises, seal up or impound the relevant assets;
(2) Order the illegal fundraiser or the assister in illegal fundraising to recover or sell at market price the relevant assets to refund the fundraising funds;
(3) Upon decision by the leading department for handling illegal fundraising of the local people's government at or above the level of a city divided into districts, notify the border inspection authority to restrict the exit of individuals who engage in illegal fundraising, or the controlling shareholders, actual controllers, directors, supervisors, senior officers, and other persons directly responsible of entities engaged in illegal fundraising.
The measures specified in items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph shall be taken with the approval of the principal head of the leading department for handling illegal fundraising.

Article 26 The sources of funds for refunding fundraising include:
(1) The balance of the illegal fundraising funds;
(2) The proceeds from the illegal fundraising funds or other assets converted from them and their proceeds;
(3) The economic benefits obtained from the illegal fundraising by the illegal fundraiser and its shareholders, actual controllers, directors, supervisors, senior officers, and other relevant persons;
(4) The illegal fundraising funds or related assets concealed or transferred by the illegal fundraiser;
(5) Economic benefits such as advertising fees, endorsement fees, agency fees, kickbacks, rebates, commissions, and royalties obtained from the illegal fundraising;
(6) Other assets that may be used as funds for refunding fundraising.

Article 37 If a functionary of a state organ commits any of the following acts, a sanction shall be imposed according to law:
(1) Knowing that the entity under his charge or supervision has suspected illegal fundraising activities, fails to handle them in a timely manner according to law;
(2) Fails to perform his duties of preventing illegal fundraising in a timely manner in accordance with the provisions, or fails to cooperate in the handling of illegal fundraising, causing serious consequences;
(3) Abuses his power, neglects his duties, or engages in malpractice for personal gain in the process of preventing and handling illegal fundraising;
(4) Supports, harbors, or connives at illegal fundraising through his official acts or by using his official influence.
If the act specified in the preceding paragraph constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law.
